m":"TEN","distance":0.6306,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennard Wilson"},{"season":2025,"team":"NE","distance":0.6324,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6402,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Giants went 4-12 in 2019 and were outscored 451 to 341. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 25th in EPA per play and the defense 24th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the 2nd highest 11 personnel usage in the league.","offense":"The Giants offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest 11 personnel usage in the league. Daniel Jones ran the passing game, with Golden Tate leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Giants defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 10th and 28th. Markus Golden led the team with 10 sacks, and Oshane Ximines added 4.5 while playing only 45% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme was fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature was a number of defensive linemen on the field that ranked 4th lowest in the league.","special":"The Giants were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 41% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 71% of field goals, which was one of the worst in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their defensive line was among the youngest in the league.","players":"Daniel Jones played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,027 yards. Targets were spread around, with Golden Tate and Darius Slayton leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Saquon Barkley handled most of the work in the backfield with 70% of the carries. On defense, Markus Golden led the team with 10 sacks and Jackrabbit Jenkins picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Colts under Frank Reich and the 2021 Steelers. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Seahawks and the 2023 Panthers under Ejiro Evero.","trends":"The offense went from 17th in EPA per play in 2018 to 25th in 2019, a big step back. The defense moved from 28th in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 24th in 2019, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was 11 personnel usage, which went from 61% to 74%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}